WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.426
Burn and inhalation injury force two different problems onto the ventilator.

2
00:00:06.426 --> 00:00:15.315
The lungs stiffen from inflammation and alveolar edema, and the chest wall stiffens from a circumferential eschar.

3
00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:27.088
On top of that, the smoke the patient breathed can carry carbon monoxide and hydrogen cyanide, which attack oxygen carriage and cellular use of oxygen.

4
00:00:27.088 --> 00:00:41.825
In this chapter we define the deck's compliance numbers, work the static and dynamic math, interpret carboxyhemoglobin, respect the limits of pulse oximetry, and run through the treatments.

5
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:46.317
Memorize the burn deck's two numbers.

6
00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:55.818
Lung compliance, meaning how easily the lungs expand, is fifty to one hundred milliliters per centimeter of water.

7
00:00:55.818 --> 00:00:59.486
Static compliance is seventy to one hundred.

8
00:00:59.486 --> 00:01:06.737
The deck calls the fifty to one hundred figure lung compliance, not dynamic compliance.

9
00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:16.655
No source anywhere gives a normal range specifically for dynamic compliance, so do not memorize that as a sourced fact.

10
00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:23.072
The weaning cutoff from chapter one is static compliance greater than thirty.

11
00:01:23.072 --> 00:01:31.323
For a burn patient, Hess's extubation criterion is respiratory system compliance of at least fifty.

12
00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:43.658
Hess's acceptable respiratory system compliance in ventilated patients is fifty to one hundred, which lines up with the deck's lung compliance band.

13
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:49.885
Compliance is change in volume over change in pressure.

14
00:01:49.885 --> 00:01:58.120
Static compliance uses the plateau pressure measured during an inspiratory pause with no flow.

15
00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.201
The formula is tidal volume divided by plateau minus PEEP.

16
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:10.210
Dynamic compliance uses peak inspiratory pressure while flow is still happening.

17
00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:17.218
Applying the deck's rule, dynamic equals tidal volume divided by PIP minus PEEP.

18
00:02:17.218 --> 00:02:28.519
Dynamic always reads lower than static when flow and resistance are present, because PIP minus plateau is the resistive pressure.

19
00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:36.667
The two values match only when flow is zero at end inspiration, as in a true descending ramp.

20
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:39.740
Take Hess's patient.

21
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:47.687
Tidal volume three hundred twenty milliliters, plateau forty, PEEP twenty six, PIP forty three.

22
00:02:47.687 --> 00:03:01.322
Static compliance is three twenty divided by forty minus twenty six, which is three twenty over fourteen, roughly twenty three milliliters per centimeter of water.

23
00:03:01.322 --> 00:03:05.922
Dynamic is three twenty over seventeen, about nineteen.

24
00:03:05.922 --> 00:03:14.873
Static is well below Hess's acceptable fifty to one hundred, so respiratory system compliance is decreased.

25
00:03:14.873 --> 00:03:26.584
The dynamic to static gap of four reflects three centimeters of water of resistive pressure, so some airway resistance is also contributing.

26
00:03:26.584 --> 00:03:35.117
Rising resistance drops dynamic without changing static; falling compliance drops both proportionally.

27
00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:41.644
The RCP 203 decks give two slightly different sets of bands.

28
00:03:41.644 --> 00:03:57.826
The common ones to memorize: one to three percent is within normal limits, five to ten percent for smokers, ten to fifteen percent for heavy smokers, and over twenty percent is CO poisoning.

29
00:03:57.826 --> 00:04:06.087
One deck prefers less than one point five percent as normal and three to six percent for smokers.

30
00:04:06.087 --> 00:04:21.418
Whitaker adds part of a symptom scale: headache, nausea, and vomiting at twenty to thirty percent, and above forty percent the patient begins losing sensorium and becomes comatose.

31
00:04:21.418 --> 00:04:35.301
The treatment end point from RCP 105 is that mild to moderate CO poisoning gets one hundred percent oxygen on a non rebreather until HbCO falls under five percent.

32
00:04:35.301 --> 00:04:49.183
The decision to go to hyperbaric is not based on the level alone: symptoms, neurologic findings, cardiac involvement, pregnancy, and the overall picture all count.

33
00:04:50.600 --> 00:05:01.306
Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin two hundred to two hundred fifty times more strongly than oxygen, forming carboxyhemoglobin.

34
00:05:01.306 --> 00:05:05.418
That drops the oxygen that hemoglobin can carry.

35
00:05:05.418 --> 00:05:17.666
It also shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve left, so whatever oxygen is still bound is held tightly and released poorly at the tissues.

36
00:05:17.666 --> 00:05:28.458
Hess adds the bigger story: unconsciousness routinely occurs at fifty percent HbCO, which shows other mechanisms are involved.

37
00:05:28.458 --> 00:05:40.363
CO binding to the mitochondrial cytochrome system, which blocks oxygen use in the cell, is likely more toxic than CO binding to hemoglobin.

38
00:05:40.363 --> 00:05:49.271
Five to twenty five percent of patients with serious exposure develop delayed major neurologic sequelae.

39
00:05:49.271 --> 00:05:53.725
CO poisoning is both an anemia and a cellular toxin.

40
00:05:55.133 --> 00:06:00.797
Measure HbCO with CO-oximetry on an arterial blood gas.

41
00:06:00.797 --> 00:06:11.301
The arterial PaO2 can be normal, because PaO2 is dissolved oxygen in plasma, not oxygen on hemoglobin.

42
00:06:11.301 --> 00:06:27.366
A standard two wavelength pulse oximeter cannot reliably tell oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin, so SpO2 reads falsely high by roughly the HbCO fraction.

43
00:06:27.366 --> 00:06:31.897
Bedside correction: subtract HbCO from SpO2.

44
00:06:31.897 --> 00:06:39.517
An SpO2 of ninety with HbCO of seven is a true saturation of eighty three.

45
00:06:39.517 --> 00:06:56.508
For moderate CO poisoning in a conscious, cooperative patient, a quick screen is exhaled CO in parts per million divided by six, which approximates the HbCO percent.

46
00:06:57.933 --> 00:07:04.185
Half-life of carboxyhemoglobin depends on what the patient is breathing.

47
00:07:04.185 --> 00:07:08.092
On room air it is roughly four to five hours.

48
00:07:08.092 --> 00:07:21.290
On one hundred percent oxygen via tight non rebreather it drops to about sixty to ninety minutes, which Hess refines to seventy four minutes on average.

49
00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:31.362
On hyperbaric oxygen at three atmospheres the half-life falls below thirty minutes, often cited as twenty to thirty.

50
00:07:31.362 --> 00:07:39.958
That is why one hundred percent oxygen comes first, and HBO is considered for severe presentations.

51
00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:48.201
Administer one hundred percent oxygen immediately, regardless of SpO2 or PaO2.

52
00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:58.891
Use a tight non rebreather at fifteen liters per minute with the reservoir inflated for a spontaneously breathing patient.

53
00:07:58.891 --> 00:08:07.215
If the patient cannot protect the airway or ventilate, intubate and set FiO2 at one point zero.

54
00:08:07.215 --> 00:08:11.421
Confirm HbCO by CO-oximetry and assess severity.

55
00:08:11.421 --> 00:08:25.528
Consider hyperbaric oxygen for severe poisoning, especially with significant neurologic depression or cardiac involvement; pregnancy also weighs in the decision.

56
00:08:25.528 --> 00:08:44.367
A typical HBO protocol is three atmospheres for the first treatment then two or three atmospheres for later treatments, each lasting ninety minutes, with two ten minute air breaks to reduce oxygen toxicity seizures.

57
00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.862
Carbon monoxide and hydrogen cyanide both come from smoke.

58
00:08:50.862 --> 00:08:58.019
In the deck's contrast, CO mainly ties up hemoglobin so blood cannot carry oxygen.

59
00:08:58.019 --> 00:09:05.351
Hydrogen cyanide forms when nitrogen containing compounds burn in a low oxygen fire.

60
00:09:05.351 --> 00:09:17.483
It binds the cytochrome oxidase system and inhibits cellular metabolism, causing tissue and systemic acidosis: the cells cannot use oxygen.

61
00:09:17.483 --> 00:09:35.725
For suspected severe cyanide poisoning, treatment is one hundred percent oxygen plus hydroxocobalamin, trade name Cyanokit, which binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, vitamin B twelve, and is then eliminated.

62
00:09:37.133 --> 00:09:49.999
Inhalation injury triggers inflammation and raises alveolar capillary permeability, which fills alveoli with fluid, drops compliance, and impairs oxygenation.

63
00:09:49.999 --> 00:09:55.861
The CXR looks like noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, an ARDS like picture.

64
00:09:55.861 --> 00:10:07.098
Then the chest wall adds its own problem: a circumferential chest burn creates rigid eschar and underlying edema that restricts expansion.

65
00:10:07.098 --> 00:10:15.566
Until a chest escharotomy is done, high airway pressures may be unavoidable to overcome the stiff chest.

66
00:10:15.566 --> 00:10:29.408
The ventilator plan is tidal volume around six milliliters per kilogram predicted body weight, plateau at or under thirty, appropriate PEEP, and lung protective strategy.

67
00:10:30.833 --> 00:10:36.485
A ventilated patient delivers five hundred milliliters tidal volume.

68
00:10:36.485 --> 00:10:41.056
Peak pressure is thirty, plateau twenty two, PEEP five.

69
00:10:41.056 --> 00:10:45.960
Pause and compute static compliance and dynamic compliance.

70
00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:52.858
Then decide whether the static value falls inside the burn deck's compliance bands.

71
00:10:53.767 --> 00:10:59.767
[6-second practice pause]

72
00:11:00.267 --> 00:11:13.640
Static compliance is five hundred divided by twenty two minus five, which is five hundred over seventeen, about twenty nine milliliters per centimeter of water.

73
00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:19.157
Dynamic compliance is five hundred over twenty five, about twenty.

74
00:11:19.157 --> 00:11:28.352
Static sits well below both deck bands, fifty to one hundred and seventy to one hundred, so the lung is stiff.

75
00:11:28.352 --> 00:11:38.549
Dynamic reads about nine lower than static because of eight centimeters of water of resistive pressure, PIP minus plateau.

76
00:11:38.549 --> 00:11:41.642
Look at both the lung and the airway.

77
00:11:43.067 --> 00:11:52.149
A patient from a house fire shows a pulse oximetry reading of ninety eight percent on a non rebreather.

78
00:11:52.149 --> 00:11:59.380
The ABG shows a PaO2 of three hundred forty and a CO-oximetry HbCO of twenty four.

79
00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:07.317
Pause and decide: is the oxygenation adequate, and what is the true functional saturation?

80
00:12:08.233 --> 00:12:14.233
[6-second practice pause]

81
00:12:14.733 --> 00:12:19.462
Oxygenation is not adequate despite the reading.

82
00:12:19.462 --> 00:12:33.449
PaO2 reflects dissolved oxygen in plasma, which CO does not touch, so a PaO2 of three hundred forty on one hundred percent oxygen is expected.

83
00:12:33.449 --> 00:12:38.572
The pulse ox cannot see HbCO and reads falsely high.

84
00:12:38.572 --> 00:12:50.097
True functional saturation is roughly SpO2 minus HbCO, so ninety eight minus twenty four, about seventy four percent.

85
00:12:50.097 --> 00:12:56.500
An HbCO of twenty four is over the twenty percent poisoning band.

86
00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:09.108
Keep one hundred percent oxygen going, trend HbCO by CO-oximetry, and consider hyperbaric therapy based on the clinical picture.

87
00:13:10.533 --> 00:13:11.390
To recap.

88
00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:25.948
The burn deck's compliance numbers are lung compliance fifty to one hundred and static compliance seventy to one hundred, with no sourced dynamic number.

89
00:13:25.948 --> 00:13:29.659
Static uses plateau, dynamic uses peak.

90
00:13:29.659 --> 00:13:36.986
Compliance falls from permeability edema in the lung and eschar on the chest.

91
00:13:36.986 --> 00:13:40.126
HbCO over twenty is CO poisoning.

92
00:13:40.126 --> 00:13:45.169
SpO2 lies in CO poisoning, so always use CO-oximetry.

93
00:13:45.169 --> 00:13:57.444
One hundred percent oxygen cuts HbCO half-life from hours to about sixty to ninety minutes and HBO drops it under thirty minutes.

94
00:13:57.444 --> 00:14:06.483
Treat suspected severe cyanide poisoning with one hundred percent oxygen plus hydroxocobalamin.
